  "result": "{\"seen_before\": true, \"matches\": [\"terminal#11149\", \"terminal#16024\", \"terminal#14100\"], \"answer\": \"This has been reported before. The cause is a bug in Windows PowerShell 5.1, not in wt.exe. PowerShell 5.1 treats the starting directory as a wildcard pattern, so it mishandles paths containing '[' or ']'. Terminal does pass the path correctly, and parentheses work because they aren't wildcard characters. The Terminal team said they can't fix this on their side (terminal#11149). Two reporters fixed it by installing PowerShell 7 (pwsh) and using it as the profile: terminal#14100 on 7.2.6, and the terminal#16024 reporter on PowerShell 7 (version not stated). Install the current PowerShell 7 and set it as your default Terminal profile. Then --startingDirectory and Explorer's \\\"Open in Terminal\\\" should open in the bracketed folder. Windows PowerShell 5.1 is unlikely to get a fix. In the meantime you can use cmd, or run Set-Location -LiteralPath after the shell starts.\"}",
